Netman Reborn is a modern recreation of two tools from the Netman suite
(Curtin University, 1993): Etherman, which shows layer 2 conversations
between MAC addresses, and Interman, which shows layer 3 conversations
between IPv4 and IPv6 addresses.  Both views are displayed side by side in
a web browser and are fed by a single passive capture.

Nodes are laid out the way the original tools did it.  Etherman places every
station on one large circle, since a layer 2 network is flat and all stations
share the same segment, while Interman draws one circle per network (classful
IPv4, /64 for IPv6) with the networks spread over a ring.  Node size follows
the cumulative byte count, edge width follows the observed bit rate, and the
colour of an edge is that of its dominant protocol.

MAC addresses are labelled with their vendor from the embedded Wireshark OUI
database, and IP addresses are renamed as soon as a reverse DNS lookup
succeeds.  Neither resolution ever happens on the packet path.  The tool is
strictly passive: it captures and displays, and never injects traffic.
